494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6fd19f6-d3a7-5978-9314-7283447a9c4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-175916" :question/problem "$$\n{{0.33\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3+1=2+2}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∧B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is true. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{0.33\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3+1=2+2}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233361df-2934-52b6-8409-87787fbc9ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389736} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfa55d8-486d-51be-a4f2-c7daf80570a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233361df-2934-52b6-8409-87787fbc9ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389736} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b17155-b098-59ce-8697-4b721be59f4e" :answer-field/key "f